 4. state tree matches the mode  [asserted]
    ✓ the TESTNET root binds, and the only delta from LIVE is the root
    ✗ 不能确立: that the tree is EMPTY of earlier runs' state
    ✗ 不能确立: that nothing outside the tree is written (absolute paths elsewhere would not show up in a single-delta check)

 5. the machine will not sleep  [observed]
    ✓ our agent holds a permanent PreventUserIdleSystemSleep, on AC, with min_evidence_h of guarded sleep-free time behind it
    ✗ 不能确立: that a CLOSED LID is safe — not established, needs a physical test
    ✗ 不能确立: that a reboot is survivable: both jobs are LaunchAgents and need a login
    ✗ 不能确立: that the machine will not sleep in FUTURE, only that it has not since the guard

 6. window start state / §4-1 collision  [observed]
    ✓ the tree's existing days and their c, the earliest window day on which the 5-day streak could complete, and that a disposition for a trip is recorded in config BEFORE any of the numbers exist
    ✗ 不能确立: that §4-1 will NOT trip — it is expected to, and no exemption was granted
    ✗ 不能确立: that the recorded disposition is the right one; it checks that a decision was written down before the data, not that it is wise
    ✗ 不能确立: anything about the OTHER six conditions' timing inside the window

 7. the anchor compiles from SOURCE  [observed]
    ✓ launchd's copy sets both PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE and a private PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX, and that directory is observed EMPTY — so every anchor compiles the source on disk
    ✗ 不能确立: that OTHER interpreters on this machine are immune — an interactive /usr/bin/python3 still caches to ~/Library/Caches and regrows entries within minutes (47 measured); only the scheduled job is covered
    ✗ 不能确立: that the source on disk is the source anyone reviewed — this makes executed == on-disk, not on-disk == intended

 8. §4-3 must be able to see (mark coverage)  [observed]
    ✓ that the +60s mark producer has caught up, so §4-3 does not enter the window reporting `triggered: False` from a tenth of its own input
    ✗ 不能确立: that the marked fills are REPRESENTATIVE — it measures how much is marked and can say nothing about the bias of which; the unmarked set is the most recent fills and the thinnest symbols, i.e. exactly where adverse selection lives
    ✗ 不能确立: that coverage will HOLD for five days: it is a reading at one instant, and the producer can stall the day after the window opens (nothing pages on that — it would surface as DEGRADED in the condition, unread until someone looks)
    ✗ 不能确立: that 0.50 is a statistically sufficient sample — it is the line below which the producer is considered BROKEN, not the line above which the estimate is sound
    ✗ 不能确立: anything about §4-3's THRESHOLD (-25bps) or its regime label, which decide what the guard judges once it can see at all

 9. write the start date  [asserted]
    ✓ the clock date is recorded, once, and cannot be silently restarted
    ✗ 不能确立: that the 30 anchors will run — that is what the window measures
    ✗ 不能确立: that anchors which DO run will trade: the clock gate and the trade gate are different questions (an off-schedule or halted anchor completes without trading, and counts as a completion)

 验收电池整体看不见的:
    ✗ no suite places a venue order; execution facts come from ops/testnet_*.py runs, by hand
    ✗ no suite runs on the launchd schedule, so nothing here proves the SCHEDULED path works — the first scheduled anchor died on a macOS permission error with every suite green
    ✗ green means 'the component behaves as specified'. Whether the specification is right, and whether anything CALLS the component, are different questions (tests_entrypoint_wiring covers the second for a few layers only)
    ✗ a suite that is green because its subject is absent looks identical to one green because its subject works — only a deliberate injection distinguishes them

 每个套件结构上看不见的一件事:
    · income_callers: that the income numbers are RIGHT — it only checks that every /fapi/v1/income caller is declared and shows structural signs of paging, dedupe and a truncation flag, and that no declaration names a caller that has gone. Four blind spots: (a) the markers are PROXIES read out of the source — a caller can show a while-loop, the string tranId and a page bound while still advancing its cursor wrongly, and this passes it; (b) it says nothing about whether the truncation flag is ever CONSUMED, which is the half that actually protects a stop-loss numerator; (c) it is scoped to one endpoint, so the identical short-read failure on userTrades, klines or any other paged endpoint is entirely outside it; (d) it cannot see a caller assembled at runtime (a path built from a variable), which would be invisible to the source scan and is exactly how the next one arrives
    · guard_reach: that the guards are STRONG — it only checks that each one has written down what it cannot stop, and that no declaration names a guard that has been deleted or renamed. Four blind spots: (a) it cannot tell a TRUE reach statement from a plausible one; nothing verifies that the named blind spot is real or that the unnamed ones do not exist; (b) it is closed over what the WATCHDOG writes, so a guard living anywhere else (an alarm rule, an executor branch, a launchd precondition) is outside the census entirely and its absence is invisible; (c) the >=80-character rule is a proxy for substance and a determined author defeats it with one long sentence; (d) declaring a blind spot changes nothing about it — several entries here name gaps that are still open, and writing them down is not closing them
    · tests_guard_reach: that the reach registry is COMPLETE — it proves the census refuses a guard with no declaration, refuses a declaration naming a guard that no longer exists, refuses a renamed symbol, and refuses an empty statement. Four blind spots: (a) its forward scan is a regex over one file, so a guard whose verdict is written through any other shape is invisible to both the census and this suite; (b) it asserts declarations are CONCRETE via a digit-or-identifier proxy, which is a heuristic and says nothing about truth; (c) the both-directions test mutates a COPY of the tree, so nothing here proves the census is wired into the battery — that is run_acceptance.sh's job and a separate claim; (d) it cannot detect the most likely rot: a statement that was true when written and quietly became wrong when the guard changed underneath it
    · tests_readout_gate: that the experiment will be read HONESTLY — it proves the gate refuses below the pre-registered anchor count, says how many more are needed and when, permits at the threshold, and that an override writes a findable audit row carrying the reason, the argv and the gate state. Four blind spots, and the first is admitted in the module itself: (a) the gate does NOT make the arithmetic impossible — per-anchor rows are printed below it because variance estimation is explicitly permitted, so a reader with a calculator can average them; it makes reading early expensive and visible, not impossible; (b) it gates a POINT ESTIMATE, and says nothing about whether the estimator itself is right — the opportunity-cost leg is not even computed yet; (c) `in_sample` is taken from the artefact, so an anchor wrongly marked in-sample counts toward the threshold and no assertion here would notice; (d) the audit row records a self-reported REASON, which is a claim by the person bypassing the gate, not evidence — argv is recorded beside it precisely because the two are different kinds of fact
    · tests_channel_causality: that the panel is LEAK-FREE — it only proves that on ONE synthetic fixture, 31 channels are invariant to poisoning all inputs strictly after a cut row, and that ch31's known leak reaches back exactly 11 rows. Four blind spots: (a) it tests the LIVE builder only — the TRAINING builder (build_wide_dl.py) is same-source but separately audited, and the causal-panel batch carries its own port of this test; (b) causality is judged against the row convention (channel[t] may use rows <= t) — if the CONVENTION itself is misaligned with execution (the bar-key T+1h subtlety), this suite blesses the misalignment; the deployment-seam audit owns that claim; (c) the synthetic fixture walks one path through the code — min_periods branches, warmup washes and real NaN patterns are not exercised, and a leak that only manifests on those paths passes here; (d) the ch31 exemption asserts the leak's REACH, not its cost — 0.079 -> 0.041 is cited from the audit, not re-established, and green here must never be read as 'the panel is clean': the frozen models still eat the leak-shaped channel by design until the retrain deployment batch deletes the exemption
    · tests_per_name_stop: that the PER-NAME STOP CLAUSE IS RIGHT — it proves the pure evaluate(): trigger at exactly 2 consecutive breaches, reset on recovery/gap/dust, flat->cooldown->expiry lifecycle, enabled=false and snapshot=None identities, and statically that the wiring text exists (broker key, both hooks, zero-before-withhold ordering, config values). Five blind spots: (a) every behavioural case runs on the PURE function — nothing proves the end-of-anchor hook is REACHED in a live anchor; an early return or exception above it leaves the clause blind with this suite green (the harvest_ema lesson, same shape); (b) the flatten action is delegated to the existing clamp pass-2 — this suite asserts target-zeroing precedes apply_withhold in TEXT ORDER only, and does not prove the stopped name actually lands in the flatten_only bucket (spec_book_reshape owns that claim); (c) the depth numerator is the venue's unrealizedProfit snapshot — a venue semantic change (e.g. folding funding into it) or a snapshot/notional caliber drift distorts depth with everything here green, W1 only proves the KEY exists; (d) cooldown is honoured via untradable->pop — a refactor that stops consuming _untradable lets a cooled name re-enter unnoticed; (e) the ECONOMICS are entirely outside: prereg cf40ea21 SS4's 30-day counterfactual backfill is a manual discipline with no assertion here, so a clause that fires too often and burns premium would stay green
    · tests_harvest_ema: that the HARVEST-SPEED TRANSFORM IS RIGHT — neutrality, unit gross, byte-identical no-op through five OFF switches, state keyed by NAME (a symbol-order permutation leaves every per-name weight unchanged), reset-on-flatten, new names starting from their own raw rather than 0, and that turnover actually falls. Five blind spots: (a) ★★★ THIS ONE MATERIALISED WITHIN 90 MINUTES OF BEING DECLARED, WHICH IS THE ENTRY'S REAL LESSON: declaring a blind spot does not close it. The wiring shipped with `_tripped = os.path.exists(_wd_path)` against a path hardcoded to the DRY_RUN tree — and all three modes' watchdog state files exist permanently carrying `tripped_at: null` (NOT tripped), so the predicate was constant True, prev was constant None, and alpha=0.3 was a byte-identical no-op while the record still said `applied: true`. It reached production (state/live/harvest_ema.json, 08:39:55Z, n_carried=0) and no reading went red. Fixed 2026-08-06 10:1xZ by extracting the predicate into `legs.harvest_reset_required` — the point of the extraction is that an inline expression inside a 300-line method is not addressable by any unit, so it could not have been covered where it was. The [W] group now pins the predicate (six cases incl. the exact regression) and the CALL SITE (two static gates: no os.path.exists predicate, and mode-rooted paths — the hardcoded state/live path also meant a DRY_RUN anchor would write into LIVE's EMA memory). The [W] group has since grown two more pins (2026-08-06 10:2xZ): the resume script must quarantine-and-remove the EMA memory (the in-loop reset only fires if anchors RUN during the trip; a stopped loop leaves pre-trip state that resume would otherwise carry across the flatten), and tests_signal_and_loop must redirect LIVE_HARVEST_STATE (measured: the battery wrote EMA state into the real DRY_RUN tree at 09:51:54Z). STILL UNCOVERED: that anchor_loop reads config/book.json harvest_ema.alpha, that it persists the state at all, and that the block is REACHED — a config key rename or an early return above it still reverts the book to alpha=1.0 with this suite green; (b) reset-on-trip is exercised by handing prev=None, never by making the watchdog trip — the detection half is untested; (c) it says nothing about whether the EMA is ECONOMICALLY right: the +0.63 net Sharpe is a replay result and this suite would pass just as green on an alpha that destroys the book; (d) the state file is JSON on disk and a truncated or hand-edited one is never fed in — the failure mode there is a silent restart from raw, i.e. degradation to alpha=1.0 with no red reading anywhere; (e) the turnover assertion runs on random walks, and the real book's reduction differs measurably (live retroactive: -88.7% vs replay -93.9%), so the number here is a direction, never a magnitude
    · tests_manifest_consistency: that the DEPLOYMENT RECORD DOES NOT LIE ABOUT WHICH MODEL IS TRADING — it proves the same three hashes stored TWICE inside checkpoints/MANIFEST.json (the `files` table and the `models` block) agree with each other AND with the bytes on disk, that the four mu/sd per-array hashes match the deployed norm_stats, and that the universe pin declares it was not re-derived for the deployed generation. It exists because MANIFEST's own first line states its purpose as 'Hashes make a silent swap detectable' and a silent swap was NOT detected: the 08-04 S2 swap updated `files` and left all seven fields of `models` describing the previous generation for two days. Five blind spots, and the first is the reason the damage was bounded rather than the reason it was safe: (a) it cannot tell you the bytes are the RIGHT model — only that three records agree; the training host never re-signed this manifest (the 08-04 amendment says so explicitly), so agreement here is self-consistency on this machine, not provenance; (b) it is a TEST, not a runtime gate — a swap performed between batteries is invisible until someone runs one, and the only runtime coverage is frozen_inputs reading the `files` table, which is why the fix was deliberately NOT a raise in load(); (c) the universe pin is asserted to be SELF-DECLARING, never to be CORRECT — the deployed generation's true member union lives on the training host and is not checked against anything here, so a generation trained on a genuinely different roster passes; (d) it says nothing about the 12 vendored source files in the same `files` table, whose drift is check_upstream_drift's claim; (e) `models` and `files` are compared to each other and to disk, but all three could be updated together to describe a model nobody intended to deploy — this suite makes divergence impossible and makes a wrong-but-consistent deployment exactly as invisible as before
    · tests_risk_budget: that the RISK BUDGET IS RIGHT — it proves the compose_book transform (sign·|shaped|^α/(σ/medσ)^λ, re-demean, L1) is neutral, unit-gross, risk-de-concentrating, sign-preserving, byte-identical OFF through five distinct switches, and numerically pinned on a hand-derivable case. Four blind spots: (a) it proves the TRANSFORM, never the WIRING — that anchor_loop actually passes rvol24 and config risk_budget is asserted nowhere here, so a preds file that stops carrying rvol24 silently reverts the book to unscaled and this stays green (the no-op fallback is deliberate fail-safe, which is exactly what makes the reversion silent); (b) α=0.5/λ=1.0 are USER-RULED numbers from a replay sweep whose IC budget is the FULL-AVERAGE caliber — 2024 alone breaches 10% and nothing here re-litigates that; (c) σ is the panel rvol_24h at the anchor row — a stale or degenerate panel produces a stale σ and every assertion here still passes on it; (d) the golden case is N=4 with one leg — interactions with funding rank-centring and the 99pct cap are exercised only through the synthetic N=60 case, not proven bounded
    · tests_static_names: that the code RUNS — it proves only that pyflakes finds no unwaived `undefined name` in signal/engine/scheduler/live/ops, plus a positive control that the gate can still see the exact 2026-08-04 shape (a local of one function referenced from another). Four blind spots, and the last is the one that matters: (a) it is not a type check and not a value check — a name that is bound and holds the WRONG object passes every assertion here, which is the sibling of the bug that motivated it; (b) pyflakes cannot see names made at runtime (`globals()[...]`, `exec`, star-imports), so a module that builds its namespace dynamically passes here and can still NameError in production; (c) the 11 waivers are argued runtime-harmless by READING the code, never by executing those branches — the 10 `fails` entries rest on 'the next statement uses FAILS unconditionally, so FAILS is bound', and the `List` entry is explicitly LATENT (PEP 526 spares it only while the annotation stays local: hoist that line to module scope, or call typing.get_type_hints() on that function, and it becomes a real NameError with a green gate); (d) ★ green here says NOTHING about the property that actually hid the 2026-08-04 defect for 16 hours — a broad `except Exception` that swallowed the raise and logged 'falling back to the existing preds file', a consequence the handler never checked and which was false. The linter finds the raise; nothing in this repo finds a handler asserting an unverified consequence, and that class remains completely uncovered
    · tests_break_decomposition: that the split is SAFE TO ACT ON — it proves dev == unauth + underfill exactly on every name, that revaluation cannot manufacture unauth, that a short-side shortfall lands in underfill, that a partial vector reports its coverage, and it replays the 04:00Z flatten to unauth-below-min-notional. Four blind spots: (a) it proves the ARITHMETIC of the decomposition, never the wiring — whether any gate reads it, and whether the answer reaches the halt, is `tests_break_split_wiring`'s claim and not this one's (this entry read 'it is RECORD ONLY, no gate reads decomposition yet' until 2026-08-03, which was true for exactly one day); (b) it establishes nothing about whether 5% is right for unauth or 40% for underfill — both are ruled numbers, and the 40% is explicitly provisional pending ten anchors of distribution; (c) `unauth` is inherited wholesale from reconcile, so an error in `expected_qty = qty(T1) + sum dq_fills` passes straight through undetected — and since the wiring, straight into a HALT decision, still with no independent cross-check; (d) the replay is ONE anchor on a three-day tree, and the tree's only non-zero unauth (18.18 USDT, the universe exits at 08-01T20:00Z) is asserted on in the wiring suite, not here
    · tests_break_split_gate: that the SPLIT IS CORRECTLY DECIDED — the pre-registered spec, written before the wiring existed. It proves pure underfill does not read as unauthorized, that pure unauthorized still trips both at the portfolio line and per name, that the real 0.002801 USDT of 1000SHIB rounding dust does not, that incomplete coverage is self-declared, and that a name the chase experiment withheld carries unauth = 0. Four blind spots: (a) every input is a HAND-BUILT decomposition dict — nothing here runs `decompose`, so a change to how the buckets are COMPUTED leaves this suite green; (b) it never calls `evaluate` or the watchdog, so it cannot see whether the verdict reaches a halt (that is the wiring suite's claim); (c) the floors are a three-symbol fixture table, so it says nothing about the real per-symbol lookup or about what happens when that lookup comes back empty and every floor silently becomes 5.0; (d) it asserts on `unauth_names` as a symbol list and would not notice if the sizes attached to those symbols were wrong
    · tests_king_cadence: that the KING LEG'S 8h CADENCE IS DECIDED CORRECTLY — it proves the phase fires refresh at 00/08/16Z and holds at 04/12/20Z, that the key is the NOMINAL 4h-grid anchor and not preds' anchor_ts_ms (which is nominal−1h: measured, the 16:00Z anchor carries 15:00Z, so hour%8 would be 7 at every refresh anchor and the cadence would never fire while every record still said 8h), that all six failure modes fall back to REFRESH, that a hold anchor does not advance the vintage, that a missed refresh expires the hold, and that the live config carries the ruled king_h=8 / phase 0 with the other legs untouched. Five blind spots: (a) it proves the DECISION and the payload mutation, never that the held vector reaches the venue — anchor_loop reads preds['king'] and nothing here asserts that path, so a refactor that reads king_fresh instead would trade 4h with this suite green; (b) it says nothing about whether 8h is ECONOMICALLY right — the +0.825 bps/anchor is a 9821-anchor replay result and this suite passes just as green on a cadence that destroys the book; (c) the phase choice 00/08/16Z beats 04/12/20Z by +0.553 bps for a reason that is GUESSED (funding-settlement synchrony) and never verified — if the true cause is something that moves, the ruled phase silently stops being the good one and no assertion here notices; (d) the hold file is JSON on disk and a truncated or hand-edited one is only exercised through synthetic dicts, never through a real corrupt file written by a crashing process; (e) ★ the staleness slack is 1h on top of the period, chosen so a single late anchor does not expire the hold — nothing here establishes that 1h is right, and a chronically late scheduler would hold opinions up to 9h old with every assertion still passing.
    · tests_k_window: that the WAIT IS THE ONE THE PROPOSAL RULED ON — it proves config/book.json carries k_seconds=900 (180 was authorized 08-10 and rolled back the SAME DAY when maker slippage measured −2.232 overturned the authorization's zero-slippage assumption; zero anchors ran at 180), that run_anchor reads that key and feeds it straight into the sleep that IS the passive window, that the fast path is DRY_RUN-only, that the executor's same-named K_SECONDS constant remains DEAD (self.k is assigned and never read anywhere in the repo), and that exactly one production file reads the key. Five blind spots: (a) it is a STATIC scan — it proves the sleep is reached in the source, never that a live anchor actually waited 900s; the pilot log is the only thing that can say that and no assertion here reads it; (b) it says nothing about whether 900 is ECONOMICALLY right — the 180 proposal's +1.8~2.2 bps simulation died on measured maker slippage, which is precisely a warning that this k's own economics rest on measurements that can move, and this suite passes just as green on a k that destroys the book; (c) the dead-constant check is a regex over one file, so a reader of self.k assembled at runtime (getattr, a subclass) is invisible; (d) shortening k changes the residual distribution the CHASE experiment measures — nothing here notices that a running experiment just changed regime; (e) the latency budget (k+400 < anchor_max_seconds) uses a hardcoded 400s overhead estimate taken from the measured 993s total at k=900, and an anchor that slows down for any other reason breaks the margin with this green.
    · tests_neutral_band: that the NO-TRADE BAND IS THE NEUTRAL ONE THAT WAS RULED — it proves the function holds in-band names bitwise (including flat names, so no dust opens), restores Σw=0 exactly by spreading the residual over the TRADED set only, passes venue-constrained names through untouched and unspread, is the identity at band<=0 (the replay fidelity gate uses the same passthrough), is wired AFTER withhold+reshape and BEFORE plan, and that the dormant bps-caliber band (executor DEFAULT_BAND_BPS=0.0) stays dormant so exactly one band mechanism is ever live — the unit-trap trio (T7) exists because 0.002 in the wrong caliber is a 10,000x-too-small silent no-op indistinguishable from today. Four blind spots: (a) all function inputs are synthetic dicts — nothing here runs a live anchor, so the first real proof the band FIRED is state/live/no_trade_band.json showing n_held>0, which no assertion here reads; (b) it proves neutrality of the TARGET, not of the realized book — downstream min-notional skips reintroduce a bounded drift (~5 USDT x n_skipped / gross) that only the reshape-anchor verification sees; (c) the economic case (+0.058 now / +0.097 post-deposit) lives in the frozen replay and this suite passes just as green on a band that destroys the book; (d) the T7 static greps pin today's spellings — a renamed constant or a refactored wiring line goes invisible, the classic green-battery-on-old-defects shape
    · tests_placement_bandit: that the PLACEMENT BANDIT RANDOMIZES EXACTLY AS PRE-REGISTERED AND NOTHING ELSE — eps=0 and every malformed config are the bitwise identity (dormant-trap discipline), assignment is a deterministic hash of (rebalance_id, symbol) so audits and replays reconstruct every draw, behind moves AWAY from the touch on both sides (a sign error would only ever produce a benign -5022 because GTX is untouched — asserted, not relied upon), shifted prices stay on the tick grid at legal decimals (the -1111 float-residue family), reduce_only rows are exempt, the behind share at eps=0.10 lands in [8,12]%, and the arm is recorded from the EXECUTOR-LEVEL experiment record never stamped on the shared plan row (the chase top-up contamination that bit three times). Blind spots: (a) no real order is sent — the actual behind fill-rate difference is what the experiment itself measures; (b) T8 is a static grep and a refactor rename goes invisible; (c) top-up rows carry the drawn arm but their price is re-quoted without the shift — analysis must filter attempt_idx==1 exactly as the prereg scopes it
    · tests_deadman_ping: that the OFF-BOX DEADMAN PING FIRES ONLY FROM THE LIVE MODE — it pins the user-provided hc-ping URL, the guard expression being byte-identical to main()'s canonical LIVE_MODE read (a second spelling is a second mode tree), every LIVE_MODE read defaulting to DRY_RUN, and the ping living inside __main__ so module-loading tests cannot ping. Exists because the ungated first version pinged from the battery's own DRY pass at 14:00Z on deploy day — a battery run would have reset the off-box clock and masked a genuinely dead anchor. Blind spots: (a) static greps pin today's spellings — a refactor that renames the guard goes invisible (green-battery-on-old-defects shape); (b) nothing here proves healthchecks actually ALERTS on silence — the check's period/grace live on the user's account, outside this repo; (c) a ping that fails silently in LIVE (network up for trading but hc-ping.com down) looks identical to a dead anchor from off-box, which errs toward false alarm, the safe direction
    · tests_ic_monitor: that the #55 REALIZED-IC MONITOR JUDGES WHAT WAS PRE-REGISTERED — it proves the thresholds are stamped (from the alpha=0.05+band offline distribution, DECIDE rarer than ALERT, both below zero), the spearman math on known-sign fixtures, the judgment ladder (insufficient<24 records-only, healthy OK, r24<p5 ALERT, r24<p1 DECIDE, pre-window rows excluded), beta causality by source order (history pushed after computation), idempotent append, and that the launchd plist exists and points at the script. Four blind spots: (a) it never reads real position_readback — a schema change upstream leaves this green while the monitor silently computes on nothing; (b) telegram delivery is untested (the import lives in a try, so a dead notifier degrades to a printed line no one reads — the silent-watcher form, partially mitigated by the DELIVERY FAILED print landing in the launchd log); (c) the thresholds' CORRECTNESS rests entirely on the offline calibration file — if the live book diverges structurally from the replay (different floors, different universe), p5/p1 stop meaning 5%/1% and nothing here notices; (d) launchd actually FIRING daily is asserted nowhere — the plist existing and the job running are different facts, and the first missed day looks identical to a healthy quiet day
    · tests_break_split_wiring: that ANYTHING ASKS the split and that the answer moves the book — it replays both directions on the real live tree (the 04:00Z anchor cleared, the 08-01T20:00Z exits newly stopped), proves three classes the guard must still stop for (the 93.46% break whose rows cannot be decomposed, the halted-but-holding anchor, the ghost book), and mutates the firing-order premise to show §4-6 re-arms itself. Four blind spots: (a) the production replays are READ-ONLY over two trees that between them contain ONE unauthorized event and ONE undecomposable break — every claim about how often either happens is outside it; (b) it never places an order or runs the ladder, so 'the halt is decided' is proven and 'the book is actually flattened' is not; (c) the alarm assertions stop at the watchdog's output — that `run_anchor` pages them, and that the page leaves the box, is asserted elsewhere or not at all; (d) the three-day underfill ledger is synthetic and coherent by construction, which is what makes it usable and also what stops it from saying anything about the incoherent ledgers production actually produces
    · tests_chase_release_check: that the chase experiment is SAFE — it proves the first-anchor release gate can go red (a tampered arm, a withheld name missing from the gap ledger, a gap filed under a borrowed reason), green, and NOT_EXERCISED, and that the third verdict is distinct from a pass. Four blind spots: (a) the fixtures are synthetic artefacts written by this suite, so nothing here proves the ANCHOR writes a block of that shape — only the wiring suite touches that, and it uses a stub broker; (b) the gate reads one anchor, so an experiment that behaves correctly once and drifts later is outside it entirely; (c) it checks that withheld names are RECORDED, never that withholding them was right — the opportunity cost the experiment exists to measure is not visible to any assertion here; (d) the inline re-derivation and chase_policy are asserted equal, which catches drift between them but cannot catch both being wrong in the same way, since one was written by reading the other
    · tests_residual_vector: that the unauth bucket is RIGHT — it proves reconcile publishes a per-name residual for every name in the readback (not only those above `tol`), signed, with a coverage denominator that can drop below 1. Four blind spots: (a) it proves the vector is PUBLISHED, never that the consumer takes it — since 2026-08-03 a halt gate reads it, and a consumer that took the anomaly list instead would leave this suite green while flooring every sub-tolerance name to zero; (b) it proves the vector reports what the anomaly list omits, never that `residual_qty = qty(T2) - (qty(T1) + sum dq_fills)` is the right definition of unauthorized — that is inherited from B30 and not re-litigated here; (c) the fixtures are two-anchor ledgers, so nothing exercises the interaction with real dq attribution across a 4-hour window, where the `_between` interval and the fill timestamps do the real work; (d) it says nothing about `underfill`, which is the OTHER half of the decomposition and does not exist yet
    · tests_chase_policy: that C is the RIGHT policy — it proves the rule computes the fill/skip split its own definition implies, on synthetic residual sets, and that ten plausible mis-implementations are each condemned by a named assertion. Four blind spots, and the first is the one that decides the candidate: (a) it cannot see OPPORTUNITY COST at all — every assertion here is arithmetic on notional and tilt, while the question 'is the alpha foregone worth more than the 40bps saved' is answerable only by actually withholding orders, which is what the randomised arms are for; (b) the band (1.5%) is a PROPOSAL, not a derived optimum — nothing here establishes that 1.5% beats 1.0% or 3.0%, and the greedy largest-first stop can overshoot flat by a whole name, which at n=2-5 names is coarse; (c) gross is held constant through the scan, so the band test carries an error of up to band*sum|r|/gross that is recorded and never asserted against; (d) the fixtures are synthetic — the real per-anchor populations are n = 4/2/5, below this repo's own not-estimable gate, so no assertion here is evidence about the LEVEL of anything
    · tests_chase_experiment_wiring: that the experiment will ANSWER its question — it proves topup() consults the policy before sending anything, that a withheld name leaves a row with a disposition cell and enters the gap ledger, and that the arm gate sits after the floor so dust is never filed as a withholding. Four blind spots: (a) the broker is a stub that never refuses and always fills, so nothing here exercises a withheld name in an anchor that then goes wrong; (b) it asserts the RECORD is written, never that anybody reads it — the arm on the row and the block in the artefact have no consumer yet, and a producer with no reader is this repo's most-repeated defect; (c) the tilt the policy decides against is a PROJECTION including from_reject residuals assumed to fill, and no assertion here covers the case where they do not; (d) it cannot see the thing the experiment costs — withholding leaves a directional gap that the next anchor inherits as a bigger delta, and neither the size nor the price of that inheritance is measured anywhere
    · tests_cond3_worst_selector: that §4-3 has anything to judge — over all history the stress-anchor count is 0, so this fix is INERT on current data. It proves the accumulator selects the worst day and that the record carries one caliber; it cannot prove the regime labelling that decides which days are stress days at all
    · tests_readiness_filters_path: that the cached filters are CORRECT — it proves the sheet looks at the file we actually write and counts real symbols. Whether those 653 entries carry the right tick/step/minNotional for LIVE is established by the venue stamp and by arm(), not here
    · tests_neutrality_check: that the book WILL be neutral — it proves only that the realised net is measured from venue truth and spoken when it leaves the band. It changes no order and closes no gap: the 16:00Z anchor would still have ended -6.9% short, we would merely have known within the minute instead of in a post-mortem. Nor does it establish that 3% is the right band — that figure is policy, not calibration, and two anchors is not a distribution
    · tests_daily_summary: that the operator will UNDERSTAND the summary — it proves the three questions are answered and that the day-boundary residual refuses rather than computes, not that the explanations land. Four blind spots: (a) most checks are substring assertions over the rendered text, so rewording a line silently breaks the guarantee while the tool still runs; (b) it verifies the numbers are PRESENT, never that they are RIGHT — a wrong wallet balance formatted correctly passes every check here; (c) the external-flow line says money is 'probably in spot' when TRANSFER is zero, which is an inference we cannot verify because the system has no spot-wallet access at all; (d) it runs on whatever the ledger currently holds, so a day with no rotation exercises none of the Q2 path and still goes green Its Q2/day-boundary prose checks are conditional on the nav-row count the tool itself reports for its `--since` window (round 5, 2026-09-10): on a state copy older than 24h they print NOT EXERCISED rather than pass or fail — so a green run on a stale copy proves nothing about those two lines. W2 2026-09-12: `account_facts` now reports realised P&L as a RECORD (USDT-only slice; non-USDT amounts listed UNCONVERTED, never summed; `realised_pnl=None` ⇒ not observable and the same-day residual refuses; a truncated income read is labelled incomplete; a pre-split row is passed through under a STATED assumption) and the per-anchor cost column uses the three buckets — both asserted on fields with the reviewer's two counterexamples as inline mutations and on real ledger rows; it still cannot CONVERT a foreign-asset fee, only name it.
    · tests_readers_three_bucket: that the COST of the book is right — it proves only that three read-only screens (daily_summary per-anchor column, first_anchor_review §3c, score_post_fix E6) now classify every fill as measured / fee-unknown / unpriced under the SAME rule as anchor_loop.neutrality_price (asserted equal key by key on shared fixtures), print a bps over the measured set only, print None with the bucket counts when nothing was measured, count an unknown fill apart from a zero fill, and that the pre-fix formulas give a number on those same rows (2026-08-05 12:00Z real anchor: 103/103 fee-unknown, old -3.70bps). Four blind spots: (a) it does not convert a non-USDT fee — a BNB commission stays UNKNOWN, so a screen can now say less than before, never more; (b) pilot_metrics.m1 (the §4-1 caliber) still keeps fee-unknown notional in its denominator at fee 0 and reads complete beside a NaN fee; it is FROZEN (check_metrics_freeze) and not changed here, so E6 derives its completeness bit from the buckets, carries m1's bit beside it with the disagreement flagged, and keeps m1's c_bps_overall as the pre-registered figure; (c) the equality assertion pins the copy in cost_buckets to neutrality_price, not to chase_readout, whose collect() needs a whole tree — the two production diagnostics are assumed to agree because the review rounds asserted it; (d) the real-ledger checks read the state copy at whatever age it is, and go NOT EXERCISED (not green) when the 2026-08-05 day file is absent
    · tests_redelivery_settles: that every alarm reaches a human — it proves the QUEUE stops asking once one has, which is a different guarantee. Four blind spots: (a) it settles on an explicit key, so a redelivery path that forgets to pass `settles` regresses silently and only the repeat count would show it, days later; (b) it does not retroactively settle the six already sent — the seventh redelivery closes the loop, so the ledger keeps six rows that look like a live defect; (c) `SUPPRESSED` counts as settled, which is right for a test harness and wrong if anyone ever suppresses in production; (d) it says nothing about whether the redelivered TEXT is still meaningful — a stale alarm replayed hours later is delivered, settled, and possibly misleading
    · tests_alarm_digest: that the right things get read — it proves the classifier separates and never demotes an undelivered alarm, not that the tiers match what a human would choose. Four blind spots: (a) the rules are REGEX over message text, so rewording an alarm silently reclassifies it and nothing here would notice; (b) `UNRECOGNISED -> DECIDE` is safe but means the tool degrades toward noise exactly as the system produces novel shapes — i.e. during an incident; (c) tier B asserts 'already decided', which is a claim about a RULING that lives outside the code — if a ruling is reversed, the tier is silently wrong; (d) it measures compression on the last 24h and reports the historical ratio ungraded, so a slow drift toward everything-is-A across weeks would not fail anything
    · tests_disposition_matrix: that no name will evaporate again — it proves every terminal state HAS a declared destination and that the ledger sizes the ones we abandon, not that the destinations are the right ones. Four blind spots: (a) the matrix is closed over `TERMINAL_REASONS`, so a leg that ends WITHOUT writing a row at all is invisible to it — and that is precisely how -5022 vanished, by never reaching a disposition; (b) it is RECORD-ONLY, so a gap being named changes nothing about whether a guard forgives it — the ledger could be perfect and §4-5e would still flatten the book; (c) the two code-dependent cells resolve on a substring of `note`, so a venue that reworded its errors would silently reclassify -5022 as a gap and -4164 as a real one; (d) `blocked_by_halt` dominating a halted anchor at ~4200 USDT is arithmetically right and operationally noisy — nothing here establishes that a halted anchor SHOULD report its whole book as gap
    · tests_reject_topup: that the book will reach its target — it recovers ONE refusal class (-5022) and makes the rest audible, and those are different guarantees. Four blind spots: (a) the recovered residual is now taken as a TAKER at a moment the market has just moved THROUGH our resting price, which is by definition the expensive moment — this suite asserts the order exists, never that the price was good, and the cost of the fix is unmeasured here; (b) -1111/-4164/-1102 clusters can still open the identical hole and by design cannot be auto-retried, so the only thing between them and another 4-5e flatten is an alarm a human must read; (c) it does not establish that 5% is the right alarm threshold — one anchor at 15.6% is not a distribution; (d) the top-up itself can fail, and a refused top-up of a refused maker leaves the same hole with two rows instead of none — better forensics, same exposure
    · tests_ops_flag_safety: that an operator cannot change production state by accident — it closes ONE tool's flag handling and enforces the class for shell scripts only. Four blind spots: (a) the closed-world scan covers `ops/*.sh` and NOT the python tools, which are safe today only because they happen to use argparse — a future `sys.argv[1]` in a state-changing python tool would not be caught here; (b) it cannot see the inverse error, a flag that IS recognised but does the wrong thing; (c) `KILL.sh` reads no arguments at all, so `KILL.sh --check` silently fires the real kill — for an emergency stop firing is the safe direction, so it is left alone, but the expectation gap that caused this incident is still present there and this suite does not close it; (d) it asserts refusal, not comprehension: nothing here checks that the usage text an operator then reads is accurate
    · tests_exit_ledger: that every position change will be explicable — it closes ONE unrecorded path (the universe exit) and proves that path end to end, from the emitted row to the real reconciler. It does not enumerate the others: any future code that moves the book without writing an order row reproduces 2026-08-01 exactly, and nothing here would notice. Three further blind spots: (a) it asserts the row EXPLAINS the change, not that the exit was the RIGHT action — a wrongly-classified name would be flattened and cleanly explained, and this suite would be green; (b) the fills.jsonl half is reachable only because the exit now carries a client id, but venue-side attribution itself is `venue_fills`' job and is not exercised here — an id that the venue never echoes back would still leave fills unattributed; (c) the fill facts come from `last_fill_details()` read one order at a time, so a broker that returned a stale answer would be recorded faithfully and wrongly, and no assertion here can tell those apart
    · tests_neutrality_price: that topping up to neutral is or is not WORTH IT — it computes one input to that decision and deliberately cannot act on it. Four blind spots, and the first two are why the number must not be read as a quote: (a) it is an AVERAGE over volume already pushed into the deficient side, so the MARGINAL cost of the top-up is strictly higher — `price_is_lower_bound` says so but nothing here bounds by how much; (b) the cost side of the comparison is measured while the BENEFIT side is not — what the residual net actually costs in P&L variance is not computed anywhere, so a ruling still rests on the vol estimate being supplied separately; (c) two anchors of taker cost (29.4bps then 54.9bps on the buy side) is not a distribution and the trend between them is not evidence of one; (d) it says nothing about whether a maker-based top-up — the mechanism most likely to be the real answer, and which belongs to aim-portfolio's tau optimisation — would cost less, because no such fill exists to measure
    · tests_reduce_only_reject: that a stuck position will be RESOLVED — it proves only that a -2022 on a tagged reduction is decided against the readback rather than assumed benign, and that the page names our book instead of our request. It changes no order and stops nothing: a genuine book/venue disagreement still ends the anchor exactly as it would have, now with a sentence attached. Three blind spots, each load-bearing: (a) the path is UNREACHABLE TODAY — reduce-only tagging only applies to withheld-AND-HELD names, and no anchor has yet had one, so every assertion here runs on fixtures and the first production -2022 will be the first; (b) it cannot tell WHICH side is wrong when the venue and our book disagree — it names the disagreement and points at our ledger first, which is a prior, not a finding; (c) the consecutive-failure TRIGGER the spec asks for does not exist and is not built here — the counter has zero readers, and this suite pins that absence as a negative fact rather than closing it, because building a new path to a halt changes a guard's consequence level and awaits a ruling
    · tests_book_reshape: that the reshaped book is a BETTER book. It proves the two shaping properties the withhold destroys are restored, that the correction is order-preserving, and that the three steps run in the order the design requires — none of which is evidence that neutrality EARNS anything. Neutrality is a risk argument, not a measured edge, and no anchor here is scored. Three further blind spots, each real: (a) the HELD-untradable branch is UNEXERCISED IN PRODUCTION — all three live anchors popped only (ARKM/PORTAL, never held), so the clamp-after-reshape residue is verified on fixtures alone and the first real one will be the first real one; (b) it cannot see EXECUTION — driving intent net to 0 says nothing about the realised net, which was -6.92% of gross at 16:01Z from maker fill asymmetry, and tests_neutrality_check owning the other half does not make the pair complete; (c) the reshape moves every weight, so it changes WHICH names clear min-notional — this proves the crossing is reported, never that the resulting trade set is the one we would have chosen
    · tests_fills_supersede: that every FUTURE reader of fills.jsonl will collapse — it pins the readers that exist today and the contract beside the writer, but a new consumer can still sum raw rows and nothing here would notice. Nor does it establish that the ledger is right: the three-way agreement (fills 3,293.97 = orders 3,293.98 = venue 3,297.30) was checked by hand once, at one anchor, and is not re-derived per run
    · tests_sheet_subject: that the readiness sheet's CONTENT is right — it proves only that the sheet names the run it judges, refuses when it cannot find that run's rows, and never renders the caller's rc as a measurement. Whether the individual checks are the right checks is a separate question, and a scoped sheet full of wrong rows would pass every assertion here. It also cannot cover a rehearsal that writes rows under a DIFFERENT rebalance_id than the one the ledger reports as newest
    · tests_param_encoding: that our orders are well-formed in general — it proves only that FLOATS leave as plain decimals at full precision. Tick/step conformance is a different property (round_px/round_qty own it, and -1111 is its symptom); this only guarantees that whatever those produce is not corrupted on the way out. The exposure it closes grows on its own: any name drifting under 1e-4 joins the class silently, and the symptom is a missing name rather than an error anyone would chase
    · tests_review_anchor_scoping: that the review screen is RIGHT — it proves only that each block describes one anchor rather than the day. The numbers themselves (net exposure, cost bps) rest on the ledger being complete and on the readback being the venue's truth; neither is established here. Its own most useful finding is negative: with ONE anchor the broken and the fixed code agree, so the first live anchor could not have caught the defect this suite exists for
    · tests_env_loading: that a page will REACH a human. It proves every module that can construct a notifier populates the credentials on import, and that the loader never overrides what the plist or the shell already set. Whether Telegram then delivers, and whether anyone reads it, is outside every test — `delivered_offbox` means the API said yes. It also cannot cover a tool that pages through some future channel that is not TelegramNotifier: the population is derived from that one constructor
    · tests_anchor_skip_visible: that an anchor will not be skipped. It proves a skip is no longer SILENT — named mode, durable record, one page — and that two scheduled anchors cannot coexist. It says nothing about the contention itself: the real contender is a human run overlapping the schedule, and no test can stop someone starting a rehearsal at :59. Nor does it change the exit code, so launchd still records a skipped anchor as a successful run — that remains true and is now merely visible elsewhere
    · tests_request_accounting: that we now know what bans us. It proves the market-data path spends the request dimension, that retries are counted, that the wallet follows the host, and that the backstop names its own state — all measured on the counters, not on the source text. It does NOT establish that our accounting now MATCHES the venue's: the 6127 weight gap was measured while two hosts shared one wallet and one of them was uncounted, so the gap has to be re-measured before any threshold rests on it. Nor does it cover the venue ORDER-COUNT/DELETE question — the timeline instrument makes that decidable, and deciding it is a later reading, not an assertion here
    · tests_book_weights_effective: that the challenger mixture is the RIGHT book to trade — it proves only that the book we send is the book config/book.json names, which is what had no guard at all. It also does not cover `signs`/`pos_cap_pct`/`funding_mode`: those are still module literals, PINNED to the file rather than read from it, so a divergence goes red but a deliberate change still needs two edits
    · tests_cancel_ban_resilience: that the exposure is CLOSED — it proves the deadline is read off the venue, that a ban longer than the phase's budget draws zero further requests, and that the unresolved orders are written down where a later process can find them. It does not prove anything acts on those pins: the only consumer today is the next anchor's stale-order sweep, so the window is still up to one anchor interval wide until an out-of-anchor sweeper exists. Nor does it establish WHY we are banned — the venue's own counter and our per-process tally disagree by ~6100 weight of unattributed traffic, and no threshold here rests on that number
