    · tests_request_identity_unknown: that the venue will ever produce these sequences — every outcome is injected. It proves, against the real submit/requote/top-up/attribution/reconcile code, that a -2013×2 'absent' is carried as ambiguous and never re-sent this anchor, that every venue request has its own client id (-1/-2/-3/-3cN) and the resolver refuses a record under another id, that an UNKNOWN request survives a later refusal and a partial attribution, that reconcile consumes the unknown as an authorised band instead of a hole, that 408/-1007/-1006 are execution-unknown, and that the cap clamp validates and applies atomically; round 4 (review 62ebf9f8): that the top-up keeps a per-request ledger in which a rejected or never-sent request contributes NOTHING to the unknown bound, that the known part keeps its sign when child fills settle it, that attribution shrinks the unknown by request identity (orderId join) and closes the leg only when no request is left unknown, that reconcile sums per-request QUANTITY intervals (Minkowski, mixed directions do not cancel, mark-independent) and treats non-finite or side-less rows as unquantifiable, that an ambiguous maker absent from a FULL allOrders page stays UNKNOWN and is settled only by its own order query (-2013 ⇒ absent), that an UNKNOWN symbol gets a bounded maker row instead of a hole, that flatten retries mint distinct ids, that legs are keyed by (symbol, orderId), and that an id over 36 chars is refused rather than truncated. round 5 (review cfaf1bbe): that the row's quantity comes from each request's own executedQty on every path (two prices ⇒ 100, never 150/2), that a child fill raises a request's confirmed quantity without ending it (band = what it may still fill; the leg closes only when every request is terminal), that contradictions (over-fill, wrong side, non-finite) are checked before any classification and keep the leg open, that child fills are de-duplicated by trade id and confirmed quantity is monotone, that an order the venue shows OPEN is cancelled and re-queried and stays UNKNOWN if still open, that cancel-unresolved symbols are UNKNOWN, that the breaker records the maker leg before refusing the top-up, that target/held are validated for every name before the cap logic, and that flatten recovery keys by (symbol, orderId) with a process-wide id counter. It does NOT prove phase B's allOrders/order reads (mocked here) resolve every leg on the real venue, nor that the venue's -2013 after the k-window is final (a stated venue-consistency assumption), nor that two PROCESSES in the same UTC second cannot mint the same flatten id, nor that the latest-only reconcile baseline carries an unexplained residual forward (registered for pre-registration, not implemented). Round 6 (review ce7b3cf4): that a later terminal fact for a request (cancel + re-query in settlement) supersedes the k-cancel's unresolved set by request and clears its pin, that a terminal request with a trusted executed quantity has no remainder whether or not its USDT amount was readable (C = Q with an unknown amount is a KNOWN quantity), that a missing executedQty is not a measured zero, that a ledger row is quantified by its ledger only (no amount-over-subset-price fallback), that the bounded reader validates its price, that a conflicting duplicate trade id is a contradiction, that an open order's executed-so-far reaches the row when its cancel fails, and that the pre-flight uses the plan's real chunk count. Round 7 (review a99c447e, from the fact table docs/DESIGN_request_fact_model): that the ORIGINAL complete_anchor chain (real broker over a scripted transport → cancel → allOrders → order query → top-up → attribution → rows) keeps a readable executedQty when the amount is unreadable (CANCELED 4 ⇒ filled_qty 4, position 10 ⇒ anomaly 6), that three explicit refusals at an unchanged position (0 and 1) leave a determined filled_qty 0 and no anomaly (the round-6 false trigger), that the decoder and the allOrders fold record contradictions (negative executedQty, explicit 0 beside a positive amount) instead of zeros, that an allOrders-terminal request resolves the k-cancel's unresolved set and pin, and that the ledger reader owns the row's columns at emission (a branch cannot clear them). Round 8 (review 82cbe018): that the submit response and the amount re-query are MERGED per field (a record lacking executedQty never revokes an earlier 20; an explicit terminal 0 read at the GET entry is a measured zero; a falling executedQty or NEW after terminal is a contradiction), that an allOrders match resolves the k-cancel's unresolved set only with an explicit terminal status AND our identity (no status ⇒ exact query; origQty/symbol/side mismatch ⇒ UNKNOWN), that a fold contradiction makes the name UNKNOWN end to end (no top-up, reader refuses), and that with a ledger the row's amount and price are the reader's (an unclosed ledger writes None and relabels). It does NOT run phase C or the watchdog itself (reconcile is called on the emitted rows), its venue is FakeNet. Round 9 (review b92e9479, DESIGN §3c): that a derived quantity or amount comes from ONE record (an amount 40 in one snapshot and a price 3 in another derive nothing — C unknown, 40 a lower bound, the row a band), that a terminal executedQty is CONSTANT (20 then 30 is a contradiction end to end; a terminal record without a C may still learn it; child fills beyond a terminal C are a contradiction), that the k-cancel's DELETE body is a record of the request (CANCELED 4 survives a sparse terminal query ⇒ top-up 6; a differing terminal 6 ⇒ contradiction, no top-up; an unreached symbol's UNKNOWN row carries the 4; -2013 after a cancel with facts is a contradiction, not absent), that a sparse terminal record (no executedQty, no cumQuote) is unreadable rather than 0, that the identity gate governs FACTS (a record failing it makes the request unmeasurable instead of entering the ledger as C 4 / terminal; a missing side, a non-finite origQty and an orderId other than the acknowledged one fail it; a side-less fill is never read as SELL), that contradictions travel with the facts on the partial path (query and page agree), and that the amount re-query's record must be OUR request (client id / symbol / orderId / origQty / side compared when the record carries them; a mismatch makes the request unmeasurable). Round 10 (review 38808cbd, DESIGN §3d): that a quantity carries its COMPLETENESS TYPE (an open snapshot's 20 followed by a terminal record without a quantity is a lower bound — [20, Q], known 70 / band 30 beside a second leg of 50, legal 70–100 CLEAN, 60 and 110 anomalies — while a terminal snapshot's 20 is final; a child-fill subset raises the lower bound and never proves the total; a child set that reaches Q does), that every UNKNOWN exit first folds the records already held (a CANCELED 4 survives an exhausted or -2013 query — the latter as a contradiction; a settlement cancel that answered CANCELED 6 survives a failed re-query; the submit response's own PARTIALLY_FILLED 4 is the first record; a status-only DELETE body proves existence and terminality, so -2013 after it is a contradiction, not absent), and that two final snapshots with different amounts contradict. Round 11 (review f79dc8cd, DESIGN §3e): that a final amount is never divided by a lower-bound quantity to make a price, that a maker leg the venue told us about carries a request ledger so its quantity is the reader's band (open 4 / final amount 6 ⇒ known 4 / band 6, total [8, 14] beside a top-up of 4), that a non-object query answer still folds the records already held, that a child subset's amount is a lower bound (the amount closes only with the set; reconcile reads a ledger row's band whether or not its amount closed), that a missing or None finality key is NOT final (no legacy fallback) while a lower bound reaching Q is final by capacity, that a top-up response without a status proves no finality, and that the submit record is bound by the client id WE sent (a response naming another id lends no orderId and fails the present-field gate). Round 12 (review 83fcbbcc, DESIGN §3f / §5 field-by-reader table): that the submit response's direct reader checks the body against the order we sent before reading a number (a body naming another client id is a contradiction, and no re-query is made under that id), that a merged record whose quantity 0 is a LOWER BOUND is compatible with a positive final amount (NEW 0 → fills → cancel with an amount-only terminal read ⇒ known 0 / band 10 / amount 4, total [6, 16] beside a top-up of 6) while a raw same-snapshot 0-beside-amount stays a contradiction, that a final amount is constant against child fills and a subset's amount may not exceed it, that a duplicate trade id without a quote never erases a recorded quote, that a child subset's vwap goes to its own column and never becomes the row's price, that an amount known beside an unknown quantity is the interval [0, Q] (known 0 + band, on the UNKNOWN row and the normal maker ledger alike — a contract choice registered in DESIGN §3f.6), and that explicit zeros carry the amount's finality. Round 13 (review ec2fe835, DESIGN §3g): that the child-fill amount LOWER BOUND (Σ over the children that carry a quote) is compared against a final amount without waiting for the others (5/N50 + 5/N-missing against a final 40 is a contradiction), that a non-finite ledger amount is flagged even when the quantity is missing, that every identity gate reads a present-but-malformed field (empty side, non-finite origQty) as a contradiction and an absent one as no evidence (one `identity_field` for the submit gate, the re-query gate and the settlement's present-field gate), and that the two cost diagnostics (neutrality_price, chase_readout.collect) measure bps over PRICED fills only and report the unpriced count / notional / fee beside it (no priced fill ⇒ None, never 0). Round 14 (review d51cb731): that an order id is a LOSSLESS integer everywhere it is read (a fraction is never truncated, ±Inf never raises through complete_anchor, text is malformed even without an acknowledged id to compare with; the child-fill joins skip and count such ids), and that the two cost diagnostics count a fill as MEASURED only with a usable price AND a known fee (fee_paid None ⇒ fee-unknown bucket, never a free fill; NaN / negative prices ⇒ unpriced), with the bps key's denominator explicitly documented as the measured fills, and that a fee written as a raw non-USDT sum without a completed conversion (fee_all_usdt False, no fee_conversion) is fee-unknown, not a known fee. Round 15 (review 1ef6ce8c): that an order id is read as EXACT text or exact value — a digit string is parsed with arbitrary precision (never through float: '9007199254740993' stays itself, two ids near 2^53 never merge, the stored value never changes), a float at or beyond 2^53 or any fractional text is malformed — through the gates, the acknowledged-id lookup and the child-fill joins (an outside fill with a neighbouring id is not attributed and does not close our open request), that the reshape report's neutrality section cites the MEASURED count and notional and explains a None price by its bucket, and that the fee value, currency flag and conversion marker on a row come from the same write (a re-stamp clears the earlier marker). It does NOT prove the venue's DELETE body always carries the final executedQty (documented, assumed), nor that two differing terminal readings can never both be genuine (a venue-side correction would need its own evidence type).
    · tests_income_twin_rows: that the venue keys income rows the way the fixture does — it proves, against an in-process fake of /fapi/v1/income, that a trade's COMMISSION and REALIZED_PNL twins (same tranId) are BOTH summed, that a row re-served across a page boundary is still counted once, and that the old tranId-only key would have dropped exactly one twin per trade (positive control). Whether the venue also shares tranIds across OTHER pairs of types, or emits a third row per trade in some product, is a fact about the venue that only the live ledger reconciliation (income vs fills) can keep proving.
    · tests_transport_resilience: that the VENUE will be reachable on the resend — it proves, against an in-process fake of urlopen, that a request-phase failure on GET/DELETE is resent a bounded number of times while an order POST is NEVER resent under any failure — transport in either phase, HTTP 5xx, 200 non-JSON — but settled only by the venue's own order record (found ⇒ adopted, -2013 twice ⇒ recorded as not sent, else ambiguous), that the requote carries ambiguity as rested and the top-up keeps a confirmed chunk and writes UNKNOWN never zero, that a still-unresolvable POST is carried as possibly-resting, that the executor records transport rows and trips its circuit breaker, and that an exception escaping the submit loop cancels what rested, writes rows and pages before re-raising. It cannot see whether the venue actually processed a request whose answer we lost when the query ALSO fails (that order is handed to phase B as possibly-resting and to the next sweep), nor whether a 0.5 s pause is long enough for the venue to have decided before we ask it, nor whether Telegram delivers the page
    · tests_stale_order_sweep: that the VENUE lists an order we left behind — the whole sweep rests on openOrders returning it. If the venue omitted a resting order, or if a fill landed between the query and the cancel, this would look identical and the stale order would survive. It closes the gap between anchors, not the gap inside one
    · tests_live_dry_pass: that the rehearsal WORKS — it has never been executed, because it needs a live key. It proves the harness reuses the existing halt, refuses the wrong account, and that the sheet does not pad itself; whether a real LIVE anchor under a seeded trip actually submits nothing is the thing the rehearsal itself is for
    · tests_setup_live_account: that the VENUE accepts these POSTs on a real account — the fake broker always does. It proves the diff, the scope, the naming of failures and idempotence; a venue that silently ignores a marginType change would look identical here and is caught only by the re-read on a real run
    · tests_live_state_root: that every CONSUMER reads its paths through state_root. It proves the mapping is right and that no key escaped the LIVE root; a module that builds `state/...` by hand would be invisible to it, and that is the failure mode this ruling chose a separate root over auditing for
    · tests_live_install_ritual: that a TESTNET install still WORKS — it is never executed here, because doing so would call launchctl load. What is asserted is that no gate was added to its branch. Nor does it prove the ritual stops a determined operator: both factors are satisfiable by one person who wants to go live, which is the point — it stops the ACCIDENT, not the decision
    · tests_gap_observer: that a gapped name is HANDLED — it is not, deliberately: the training panel shares the wash convention, so the name keeps its weight and this only makes the event visible. Nor does it cover the real venue path: the count is asserted on a synthetic CLOSE, and whether live_panel leaves NaN in CLOSE for a genuine halt is untested here
    · tests_alert_tiers_live: that the page is DELIVERED. It proves run_anchor can import its alarm module in the real launchd context and that the dedup key varies — neither of which says a human received anything. Delivery is notify_audit's delivered_offbox, and nothing here reads it
    · tests_reprice_day: that the venue's income numbers are RIGHT — it tests the script's four refusals, not the endpoint's honesty. Nor does it cover the caliber difference it documents: a repriced row closes its window at 24:00Z while every normally-written row closes at the anchor that wrote it, and NOTHING compares the two
    · tests_stuck_position_ack: that anyone will ever REVOKE an ack. The qty pin makes an ack expire when the position changes, which is the automatic half; a residual that stays exactly put stays acked forever, and only a human re-reading the every-anchor report catches that. The report line is the guard, and nothing tests that a human reads it
    · tests_flatten_cancels_first: that the venue actually cancels when asked — it asserts we ASK, and that a refusal is named. The 4-hour observation gap between anchors is unchanged and unfixable by any guard: it is registered as an accepted in-window risk, and the real answer (a websocket user-data stream) is post-window
    · tests_halt_verdict_states: that the halt fields are correctly WRITTEN — it asserts the verdict reads them. If run_anchor stamped watchdog_halt wrongly, every verdict here would be confidently wrong in the same direction
    · tests_halt_evidence: that the halt was ENGAGED at the anchor it reads — it reads what the anchor LEFT BEHIND. It also cannot close B7's fourth quantity: on a flat book reduce-only reachability is NOT_OBSERVABLE by construction, and that honest refusal is the thing under test. The behaviour half lives in tests_binance_broker [B]/[C]
    · tests_threshold_roles: the FREQUENCIES the rulings quote (3 in 4.5y / ~1.8 a year). Those are properties of the historical series, measured in the STRUCTURAL caliber, so the real alert rate can only be higher. This proves the two levels are wired to different consequences and that the retired 5x constants are recorded but never compared
    · tests_request_budget: that 600 requests/min is the RIGHT cap — the venue named 6000 without a window, so the number is conservative under any reading and is not a measurement. It proves the dimension is counted and throttles; it cannot prove the venue has no fourth quota we are still blind to
    · tests_notional_backfill: that the venue reported every child fill — it proves the amount is rebuilt from the children we hold, signed, and only when the column is empty. If userTrades itself is short the recovered figure is short with it, and the row would then read as a confident wrong size rather than an honest None
    · tests_sizing_policy: that 2.0x / +-10%% / 4,000 USDT are the RIGHT policy numbers — they are the user's risk tolerance and no suite can settle them. It proves the sizing arithmetic, the dead zone, the floor-halts-not-shrinks rule, the dead-zone x floor interaction, and that the threshold move is exactly x(2/5) of the 5x originals. It does NOT prove the historical series that justified the restatement is representative — that series is the structural caliber and is a one-sided floor on dispersion
    · tests_orphan_position: that a withheld-and-held name will actually be REDUCED at the venue — it proves the target survives the clamp so plan() emits a row, not that the order fills; a venue that refuses reduce orders too would still strand the position
    · tests_frontier_staleness: that the frontier marker the upstream publishes is CORRECT — it proves we consume it and that a frozen frontier is no longer a pass. If the producer published a wrong newest_scored_anchor this consumer would believe it
    · tests_head_liveness: that a head which passes the liveness test is USEFUL — it proves a collapsed head is caught and that the verdict is scale-free, not that a live head carries signal. IC is what says that, and no liveness test can
    · tests_universe_tripwire: that the frozen model actually saw every pinned symbol in TRAINING — the union was determined from a live-extended REBUILD, so it may be wider than the panel the training runs consumed, and that error is one-directional: it makes the guard LESS likely to fire. Only the training host can close it. It also cannot show the guard catching a monthly ROTATION: while the column set is frozen, members are a subset of the union by construction and OK is structural. That is the finding, not a gap in the suite
    · tests_fee_asset_detection: that NON-USDT FEES ARE MEASURED AND ALARMED PROPORTIONATELY — [A-C] the column takes all three states from the real commissionAsset; [D] the per-LEG BNB→USDT conversion (c05f1f5) reaches the stamp and M1, so a converted fee is measured in full and a known-incomplete one is refused, never zero; [E] the alarm reports CHANGES of the fee-asset set, not its state (2026-08-06 re-tier, user-ruled: BNB fees are deliberate config — the old alarm paged HIGH six times in 6h for one known fact, and its text was FALSE post-conversion), with incomplete conversion still paging every anchor. Five blind spots: (a) the conversion price is a collect-time bookTicker mid, not the fill-time price — a fast BNB move between fill and collect misprices the fee by that drift, and nothing here bounds it; (b) the [E] change detection is SET-based: BNB's SHARE can swing arbitrarily without a page so long as the set {BNB} is unchanged — a slow depletion shows up only at the cliff, not on the slope; (c) the baseline is a file a human or a tree reset can delete, costing one spurious first-seen page (fail-noisy, by design); (d) the wiring checks are substring-grade statics over anchor_loop — they prove the call and the env override EXIST, not that the branch is reached on a real anchor; (e) baseline reads are not cross-mode-guarded (worst case: one wrong page after a mode mixup, judged not worth the read_stamped ceremony — revisit if a wrong-mode page is ever observed)
    · tests_reconcile_severity: that the 5%% band and the 5 USDT floor are the RIGHT numbers — it proves the grading separates revaluation from structural difference (sign flip, one-sided name) and that venue truth is still adopted unconditionally. It cannot separate the two EXACTLY, because the cache stores no mark; a name whose price moved >5%% between anchors will be graded REAL and is a false page by construction
    · tests_numerator_honesty: that the venue's income ledger is COMPLETE — it proves we page it, do not double-count colliding timestamps, and that a truncated day is withheld rather than priced. It cannot prove the venue reported every settlement in the first place, and it says nothing about whether -6.7%/-6.0% are the right limits: this batch repairs the numerator and deliberately leaves the thresholds where they are
    · tests_external_book: that the WIDE BOOK IS WORTH TRADING — it proves the adapter reads the producer's signed target exactly (in-universe w / in-universe sum|w| x NAV x gross_mult, bitwise on a neutral unit-gross fixture; no EMA, no band, no risk budget), that every named defect of the file (missing / sidecar missing / sha mismatch / bad json / schema incl. a missing universe list / universe-list sha / anchor mismatch / stale / future-dated / pin mismatch / bad weights / gross_norm mismatch) HOLDS the anchor with zero orders and a HIGH `external_book_unavailable`, that the hold never falls back to the internal composer, that the age feeds the pre-registered ladder (and `on_unavailable: hold` pins it), that internal mode is byte-identical (the external reader is never invoked; a mutant that forces the external branch goes red), that INVALID config blocks, that names outside the producer's universe list are never targets (popped; the book normalised by the in-universe sum so the live gross is undiluted; a >25% tail pages HIGH; a reader that does not split is a red mutant), that a HELD name the file no longer targets is exited reduce-only through clamp/flatten_only (never market-exited), that the two KEPT per-name filters (venue meta: non-COIN/non-ASCII/non-USDT/leveraged/non-TRADING; 2x min-notional) withhold exactly the named names, and that the per_name_stop profile switch resolves (null = base bitwise, wide = d30_n2_c42, unknown = base + alarm) and is coupled to book_source; every fixture is the REAL config pinned to the internal baseline, so the suite is green with the disk in either state. Seven blind spots: (a) every loop test runs DRY_RUN with a stub bookTicker and hand-set filters — no venue, no fills, no real NAV; the first LIVE external anchor is the first; (b) the wait-for-slot is tested as a pure plan (wake time, 0/positive) — whether the real process actually idles to N+offset and the 3000s cap suffices is a wall-clock fact only a scheduled anchor shows; (c) it proves the file is READ FAITHFULLY, never that the producer's weights are RIGHT — the wide book's alpha, turnover and the N+8/N+23 phase question live in the shadow/audit, not here; (d) the producer side (shadow_loop_v3.write_target_live) is tested in the research repo (tests_target_live_output), and the pair agreement (its output is accepted by this reader; a v2-style file is refused) is asserted THERE, not in this battery; (e) venue eligibility is a pure function over a synthetic exchangeInfo — the real payload's field names drifting (underlyingType) would exclude nothing and this stays green; (f) the 2x min-notional rule, the 10% breadth-loss line (EXT_DUST_ALARM_FRAC) and the 25% tail line (OUTSIDE_UNIVERSE_ALARM_FRAC) are policy numbers — none is calibrated here; (g) the universe LIST is trusted once its sha matches the declared universe_sha — whether it is the RIGHT universe (450 = symbols_live) is the producer's claim, pinned only if `universe_sha_pin` is set
    · tests_guard_calibers: that the guard reads the ACCOUNT — it proves the §4-2/§4-4/§4-4b/per-name/§4-1/3/5/6/7 arithmetic on fixtures generated from the producer's own identity (nav_t = nav_{t-1} + d_realised + d_unrealised + d_flow) and that 33 named defects (the retired double-count caliber among them) turn it red; it CANNOT prove daily_nav's producer matches the exchange (that is guard_twin's job, off-battery), does not cover §4-5d (not in the real-time layer), the 5c error-code fast path (no producer) or a 5e mutant (tests_break_*), and does not claim cut-invariance ACROSS a transfer day (B33 prices it as a daily approximation by design).
    · tests_position_break_blindspot: that 25%/10% are the RIGHT thresholds — it proves the gate judges halted anchors against the correct intent (flat), that the self-lock bound holds and is N-dependent, and that arming it leaves §4-5b byte-identical. The threshold VALUES are a measurement on the production tree, restated in position_break.py and adjudicated by the lead; no suite can settle them. It also runs against the LIVE ledger, so its fixtures grow over time — a future regression could be masked by newer clean anchors
    · tests_trip_page: that the page is DELIVERED — it composes severity and wording from the ladder's facts; whether the notifier reaches a human is B3's question
    · tests_sigma_ladder: that a dashboard-written gross-ladder state file can only LOWER exposure when it is fresh, well-formed, whitelisted and hash-consistent — every defect (missing, stale, tampered, out-of-range) falls back to g=1.0; the arithmetic contract target_leverage = gross_mult x g
    · tests_requote_experiment: that the direct arm is CHEAPER or DEARER — it proves only, on a fake broker, that the arm assignment is deterministic and reproducible from (rebalance_id, symbol), that p=1.0 and every config/exception failure are bitwise the current behaviour (requote all), that a direct-arm name is never requoted, stays in benign_rejected with from_reject and triggers no venue call when it is the only candidate, that exempt (reduce_only) names are requoted, that rows and requote_report carry the arm, and that config/book.json holds the key. The economics are the experiment's own reading (PREREG_requote_randomised, once, at n>=1,500)
    · tests_reject_rate: that the venue REFUSED anything at all — it proves only, on fixture rows and plans, that reject_rate.summarise puts the answered first-attempt makers in the denominator (refused-at-1 + rested-at-1), counts a twice-refused name once, keeps skipped rows and rested requotes out of the denominator, splits -5022 from the non-recoverable codes, reproduces the legacy ratio (87/187 = 46.5% vs true 67/158 = 42.4% on 09-05 08Z), and that _trade is wired to it. Whether -5022 is CAUSED by quote staleness is a live-log finding (E-0905-H), not something this suite can establish
    · tests_markout_import: that the archive-derived marks are CORRECT — it proves only, on a temp ledger and an in-memory marks dict, the supersede semantics of ops/import_markout_marks.py (ok mark supersedes pending and expired rows, venue marks win, no-trade becomes terminal, archive_missing is skipped, idempotent, dry-run writes nothing). The mark values themselves were validated on the pod against re-derived archives (20/20, 10/10), not here
    · tests_markout_window: that the +60s mark is now COMPLETE or unbiased — it proves only, on a fake broker and a temp ledger, that a trade within the 60s window resolves with its lag recorded, that a fully-returned empty window writes a terminal row that leaves pending_rows(), that a truncated group still falls back per target, and the budget constants (900 requests / 1800 s / PACE_S 2.0). It does not exercise the launchd schedule, the real venue, or whether the widened window changes the markout statistics (mark_lag_s is recorded so a reader can restrict to ≤5s)
    · tests_markout_pacing: that the venue's 1-minute weight window actually stays under the ban line — it proves only the metronome arithmetic inside _Budget.take() on a FAKE clock (no two takes closer than PACE_S, a wait that would cross the deadline is refused, sub-budgets share one metronome, the request budget is spent once per take). It does not exercise a real sleep, the launchd between-anchor run, the venue's window alignment, or the weight of any other request class; the proof that the fix worked is the next anchor's rate_timeline (venue header peak_used_weight_1m ≤ ~1200), and a regression in the broker's weight table would pass this suite unseen (E-0903-A).
    · tests_topup_leg_fill: that the venue's fill numbers are right — it proves a leg reports its OWN execution, not that the execution was read correctly
    · tests_flatten_ladder: that a real venue accepts the contract quantities; the mock cannot reject, so only the SUBMITTED VALUE is under test, never the outcome
    · tests_readback_universe: that the venue's position list is complete — it proves a zeroed departee gets a row, not that the venue told us the truth
    · tests_market_maxqty: that MARKET_LOT_SIZE is the ONLY binding cap; other filters (notional caps, position limits) are unexamined here
    · tests_flatten_rows: that the ladder's rows are RECONCILED correctly — it proves they are written and classified; what §4-5b does with them is reconcile's test
    · tests_scoped_writes: that no OTHER tool writes into the log; it proves the watchdog evaluation does not, for the one caller shape the scorer uses
    · tests_m4_rowtype: the DILUTION case — removing the guard makes m4 raise, so the counterfactual `float(x or 0)` path is argued against, never exercised
    · tests_drift_gate: that the A-set is the RIGHT set — it proves the declared set is covered, that an empty or short manifest is a RED, and that the drift and freeze guards pin identical bytes; the membership decision lives in docs/VENDORED_MAP.md
    · tests_persistence_and_dust: whether 5 USDT is the right floor or 3 consecutive days the right window — it proves dust does not fire, 6 USDT does, and that a halted day cannot feed the condition holding the halt
    · tests_read_failure_visibility: that the classes are the RIGHT ones for a real venue — it proves distinct failures stay distinct and that an exhausted retry is still UNKNOWN, using injected errors, not observed ones
    · tests_acceptance_entrypoints: that the suite LIST is correct or complete, AND — when run from inside the battery — the byte-identical-output half is SKIPPED (it would re-enter the battery); in-battery this suite certifies only the structural one-list property
    · tests_ghost_rows: contamination that carries a submit_ts — the signature is 'never left this process', so an artifact that looked submitted would pass through
    · tests_imports: that a module which imports also WORKS — it is deliberately dumb
    · drift_gate: upstream changes that are semantically new but shaped like the old data
    · tests_binance_broker: real venue behaviour: every response here is constructed by us
    · tests_binance_executor: whether the venue accepts the rounded sizes it produces
    · tests_binance_funding: whether fapi's live funding schema still matches the fixtures
    · tests_watchdog: whether anything CALLS the watchdog (that lives in the entry point)
    · tests_pilot_log: whether the anchor actually hands it rows to write
    · tests_signal_and_loop: model quality — it checks composition and control flow, not alpha
    · tests_entrypoint_wiring: layers below the few tokens it greps for; it is a wiring check
    · tests_telegram_notify: whether a message is DELIVERED off-box (the audit counter does)
    · tests_inference_parity: that the frozen model is still the right model to be running
    · tests_panel_build: live fapi data quality — parity is against a frozen server panel
    · tests_factor_health: whether the factor is decaying, only whether decay would be noticed
    · tests_state_root: writes made through absolute paths that bypass the root entirely
    · tests_nosleep: this machine's real power state — it runs on synthetic observations
    · tests_venue_fills: whether the collector is CALLED by the anchor — it tests the collector, not its wiring, which is exactly how the doubling defect survived
    · metrics_freeze: whether the frozen version is CORRECT — a hash certifies identity, not quality, so a frozen bug stays frozen; it also says nothing about the OTHER code a number passes through (watchdog, the loop), only about pilot_metrics.py
    · tests_artifact_assertions: whether the assertions are the RIGHT ones — it checks that the classifier separates structural constancy from a missing writer, and that a failure line carries what it saw; it says nothing about whether the set of assertions covers what an anchor should leave behind, and a trace nobody asserted on is invisible to it exactly as before
    · gate_coverage: whether the boundaries WRITTEN here are the true ones; it checks that every step and suite HAS one, never that the sentence is right
    · tests_score_anchor_selection: whether the SELECTED anchor is a good exam subject — it proves the scorer picks the newest scheduled rebalance rather than a ladder batch, and says nothing about whether E1-E6 are the right questions or whether their thresholds are calibrated (E5's caliber defect, found the same night, is invisible to it)
    · tests_cond6_eligibility: whether the 0.85 LIMIT is the right limit, and whether the metric is the right metric; it proves eligibility is decided per anchor by `submitted > 0` and that the halted/all-rejected pair cannot be separated by value — it does not check that weight fidelity is what §4-6 should be measuring
    · tests_venue_error_classification: whether the four verdicts are the RIGHT verdicts — a classification decision is a judgement and this suite only pins the one that was made, plus the agreement between table and classifier. It also cannot see codes the venue has not yet sent us, which is the table's designed-around incompleteness
    · tests_universe_guard: whether an OOD member is HARMFUL — it proves an unseen coin is named rather than scored in silence, and says nothing about how wrong the prediction is, nor whether the pinned training union is itself correct. It also cannot check membership at all until that union is computed on the training host and pinned
    · tests_fill_collection_gap: whether the fills can be RECOVERED — it fires the moment orders reached a terminal state with no fills collected, and says nothing about why the collector came back empty or whether a backfill exists. It is a detector for a loss, not a repair of one; B26b is the repair
    · tests_flatten_record: whether the ladder's flatten actually EXITS the book — it proves the flatten leaves a record with the same answers a rebalance leg gives (who said what, and what we hold afterwards), and says nothing about whether the exit succeeded, was fast enough, or was priced well. It also cannot see a position change made by a path outside this repo (a hand-placed order, a venue liquidation), which remains the residual half of the B24 blind spot
    · tests_nav_staleness: whether the equity snapshot itself is RIGHT — it proves §4-2/§4-4 read the day's LAST row rather than one captured before the day, and says nothing about whether the venue's equity figure is accurate or whether `realised_pnl` was computed over the correct window. It also does not cover the ACROSS-day question: how an overnight unrealised loss should appear in the D+1 row is deliberately unanswered until the first UTC-midnight crossing is observed
    · tests_frozen_inputs: whether the pinned bytes are the ones TRAINING used — a pin written here can only detect a change from what this machine runs, and every census row says so in `establishes`. It also cannot enumerate an artifact that lives outside config/ and checkpoints/ (a model input reached through an environment variable or an absolute path would not appear in the completeness sweep), and it cannot supply the training member union, which only the training host can compute
    · tests_fill_backfill: whether the venue still HAS the trades — it proves the recovery path is attributable (venue-side leg rebuild), idempotent by trade id, refuses to write rows it cannot attribute, and counts the `orders` rows it deliberately does not touch. It cannot prove anything about retention windows (userTrades/allOrders reach only so far back), and it does not repair `filled_notional`/`fee_paid`, which need a correction row type
    · tests_reconcile_qty_caliber: whether the venue's QUANTITY is itself right — it proves the comparison is made in contracts and is therefore blind to price moves, and it takes `venue_position_qty` and `filled_notional/avg_fill_px` at face value. It also cannot see the rows it refuses: a pre-B30 readback (no quantity, non-zero notional) is counted as `unreconcilable` and NOT compared, so for those anchors §4-5b is honestly silent rather than clean. The residual carries the rounding of `avgPrice` to the symbol's price precision until a `filled_qty` column exists
    · tests_avgpx_backfill: whether the CHILD FILLS themselves are complete — it proves both forms of the write-back (the rebalance leg's missing price, the flatten leg's missing amount) reproduce the venue's own Σnotional/Σqty identity, stay signed, and leave a null where the join is not exact; it takes the orderId attribution as the truth about which fills belong to a leg, so an unattributed child yields a figure over a SUBSET and the suite cannot tell that from the whole. It also cannot prove the flatten half against real recorded fills: flatten children are not written to any table of ours, so the fixture SUPPLIES them (constrained to the venue's own position change, and run at two price splits to show the §4-5b verdict is price-independent). And it says nothing about the third form of the same omission, still open: `executedQty` is read on both paths and never written, so `filled_qty` — the column `reconcile._exec_qty` prefers — has no producer at all
    · tests_guard_coverage: whether the coverage FLOOR is the right floor, and whether the mark itself is right — it proves §4-3 reports DEGRADED/BLIND instead of a bare pass when it cannot see its own input, that a breach still fires through a thin measurement, that the exit's fee is None rather than 0 when nothing was read, that the backfill's budget is spent in requests and its days served oldest-first with a share each, and that a readable quantity with an unreadable amount reaches the top-up as UNKNOWN. It does NOT establish that 0.50 is the correct coverage floor (adopted 2026-07-28 as the line below which the mark PRODUCER is considered broken — not as a sample-size argument — and frozen for the window), nor that the marked subset is representative — the suite measures how MUCH is marked and can say nothing about the bias of WHICH fills are marked, which is the thing a low coverage actually endangers. Its backfill fixtures also serve aggTrades from a mock, so they cannot show the real venue's truncation or empty-window rates, only that both branches behave when they occur
    · tests_rehearsal_anchor: whether a rehearsal ACHIEVES its declared target — it enforces the R prefix, the three refusals and the counting exclusions, and never asks whether the never-executed path actually got executed. It also cannot prove the BOOK-derived gates are unaffected, because they must be affected (constraint 5)

(覆盖 9 个门步骤 / 133 个套件, 全部有边界自述)
